  "query_preview": "..\n\nHigh pressure will center over the Great Lakes this morning and \nafternoon, supporting a largely dry forecast. A weak mid-level \nshortwave will traverse the area early this evening, bringing a very \nlow chance to generate an isolated showers across the Tri-Cities to \nThumb. Weak mid-level capping and negligible instability keeps the \npossibility of this low. High pressure to shift into New England \ntomorrow as mid-level flow becomes increasingly zonal with little \nforcing locally. This will support dry conditions through much of \ntomorrow. \n\nAttention will turn upstream tomorrow night as well established \nsoutherly flow across the Plains helps initiate and support sustained \nconvection. This activity will likely progress eastward as an MCS \ntowards the Great Lakes. As is often the case with these systems, \nconvection will encounter little instability and weaker shear with \neastward extend, favoring a reduction in both intensity and coverage \nbefore reaching SE MI. Some remnant shower or thunderstorm activity \ncould survive into the area Sunday, but confidence in timing and \ncoverage remains low. \n\nOf potential greater consequence through the Sunday to Monday \ntimeframe will be the placement of an increasingly defined low-level \ntheta-e gradient/baroclinic zone across the Great Lakes. Current \nguidance focuses the stronger isentropic ascent and associated \nprecipitation across northern lower Michigan, clipping portions of \nthe northern portion of the forecast area, leaving much of SE MI \ndry. This scenario is reflected in the very warm NBM temperature \nforecast, with temperatures peaking in the mid to upper 80s Sunday and\nup to 90 in the urban areas by Monday. Confidence in temperatures at\nthis range are considerably lower than these values would suggest as\nonly a modest southward displacement of the projected \nascent/precipitation would introduce substantially greater cloud \ncoverage and potentially prolonged rainfall across a larger portion \nof the cwa. This southward shift could hold portions of the region in\nthe 70s to low 80s Sunday and establish a much sharper north-south \ntemperature gradient on Monday. \n\nThere is slightly better convergence between model output of a more \nnorthward advancement of the theta-e gradient Tuesday as southerly \nflow increases warm advection into the Great Lakes which would then \nfavor well above normal temperatures. But overall next week does \nhighlight the potential for a more active period of showers and \nthunderstorms and increased flood risk should the convergence zone \nfail to make appreciable northward progress by the midweek period.",
